                }
              ],
              "notes": [
                {
                  "type": "Paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Note",
                      "id": "table1fn1",
                      "noteType": "Footnote",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "Text",
                              "value": {
                                "string": "Timepoints are relative to when galactose was added."
                              }
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Note",
                      "id": "table1fn2",
                      "noteType": "Footnote",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "Text",
                              "value": {
                                "string": "A one-way ANOVA was used to test for significant differences."
                              }
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Because TIR1-mediated degradation of Rad9-AID even without auxin caused most cells to adapt 24 hr after inducing DNA damage, we asked whether overexpression of "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "RAD9-AID"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " could overcome this effect. We added a "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "TRP1"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " centromere-containing plasmid copy of "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "RAD9-AID"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " with its endogenous promoter (pRAD9-AID) to our 2-DSB "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "RAD9-AID"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " strain. Degradation of Rad9-AID by IAA 15 hr after DSB induction did not trigger release of cells from G"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Subscript",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "/M arrest ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Figure 5E"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#fig5",
                  "jatsRefType": "fig"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ", "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Table 1"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#table1",
                  "jatsRefType": "table"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). However, unlike degradation of Ddc2-AID or Rad53-AID2 in this same situation, Rad53 remained hyperphosphorylated up to 9 hr after adding IAA ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Figure 5E"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#fig5",
                  "jatsRefType": "fig"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). Therefore, while the DDC proteins Ddc2, Rad9, and Rad53 are required for the maintenance of checkpoint arrest at early stages, surprisingly, they are dispensable for prolonged arrest following induction of 2 DSBs. These results suggest that prolonged cell cycle arrest is maintained by signaling proteins other than the Mec1 branch of the DDC."
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Section",
          "id": "s2-6",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Heading",
              "level": 2,
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Spindle assembly checkpoint proteins Mad1 and Mad2 are required for prolonged arrest"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "In addition to the DDC, the SAC maintains genomic integrity by halting mitosis at the metaphase/anaphase transition in response to unattached kinetochores, to ensure accurate chromosome segregation (reviewed by "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib34",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "McAinsh and Kops, 2023"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). We have previously shown that inactivation of the SAC by a "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "MAD1, MAD2,"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " or "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "MAD3"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " deletion shortened the duration of the cell cycle arrest induced by a single DSB ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib11",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Dotiwala et al., 2010"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). To test whether SAC is involved in enforcing and sustaining permanent cell cycle arrest in response to 2 DSBs, we deleted "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "MAD2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " in the 2-DSB strain. Adaptation assay results illustrate that nearly all "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "mad2∆"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " cells arrested at 4 hr but began to adapt between 12 and 15 hr after DNA damage ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Figure 6—figure supplement 1A"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#fig6s1",
                  "jatsRefType": "fig"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). To explore whether deletion of "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "MAD2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " can antagonize the permanent cell cycle arrest due to hyperactive DDC signaling, we overexpressed Ddc2 in "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "mad2∆"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " 2-DSB cells and assayed mitotic progression. We find that both in 1-DSB and 2-DSB strains, "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "MAD2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " deletion leads to cell cycle re-entry even when Ddc2 is overexpressed ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Figure 6—figure supplement 1B"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#fig6s1",
                  "jatsRefType": "fig"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). Based on these data, we concluded that mitotic inhibition is enforced by the SAC proteins as DDC factors become dispensable 12–15 hr after the induction of damage."
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "If SAC proteins are only required at later stages of cell cycle arrest when DDC proteins are dispensable, then the depletion of SAC protein Mad2 or its binding partner Mad1 soon after the induction of DNA damage should not affect DDC or cell cycle arrest up to 12–15 hr. In agreement with this idea, after Mad1-AID or Mad2-AID depletion at 4 hr, cells remained arrested up to 15 hr following DSB induction, with persistent Rad53 hyperphosphorylation ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Figure 6—figure supplement 2"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#fig6s2",
                  "jatsRefType": "fig"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). Strikingly, these cells eventually re-entered the cell cycle by 24 hr (20 hr after depletion of Mad1 and Mad2). It is notable that cells resumed cell cycle progression despite persistent Rad53 hyperphosphorylation. This result reinforces our conclusion that Mad1 and Mad2 are not required for the activation and initial maintenance of arrest but are essential for prolonged cell cycle arrest in response to 2 DSBs. These data also suggest that in the absence of Mad1 or Mad2 cells become insensitive to the arrest normally imposed by DDC."
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "To monitor the effect of SAC proteins Mad1 and Mad2 at late stages of prolonged cell cycle arrest when DDC signaling becomes dispensable, we depleted Mad1-AID or Mad2-AID 15 hr after DSB induction. Following the depletion of Mad1-AID and Mad2-AID, we observed an immediate reduction in the percentage of G"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Subscript",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "/M-arrested cells ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Figure 6A–C"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#fig6",
                  "jatsRefType": "fig"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). DAPI staining confirmed that Mad1-AID or Mad2-AID-deplete cells re-entered the cell cycle, evident from an increase in the G1 cell population as well as an increase in percentage of large-budded cells with 2 DAPI foci ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Figure 6B and D"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#fig6",
                  "jatsRefType": "fig"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). Similar to what we observe following Mad1-AID and Mad2-AID depletion 4 hr after damage, this cell cycle re-entry occurred despite persistent Rad53 hyperphosphorylation ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Figure 6A and C"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#fig6",
                  "jatsRefType": "fig"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ")."
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Figure",
              "id": "fig6",
              "label": "6",
              "labelAutomatically": false,
              "caption": [
                {
                  "type": "Paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Strong",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Text",
                          "value": {
                            "string": "Degradation of Mad2 or Mad1 at 15 hr releases cells from checkpoint arrest. "
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
