        {
          "type": "Section",
          "id": "s2-8",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Heading",
              "level": 2,
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "The location of the second DSB site relative to the centromere affects prolonged arrest"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "In contrast to the permanent cell cycle arrest we observe in response to 2 DSBs, a recent study using two HO-mediated persistent DSBs showed that cells in fact adapt ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib50",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Sadeghi et al., 2022"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). One difference between these two 2-DSB systems is the relative position of the DSBs, which might affect how SAC components become engaged, and thus might determine the extent of mitotic arrest. Supporting this, we previously showed that deleting "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "CEN3"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " in a strain with a DSB at "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "MAT"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " on chromosome III eliminated the Mad2-dependent delay in adaptation, but deleting "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "CEN3"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " when the DSB was on chromosome VI had no effect ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib11",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Dotiwala et al., 2010"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). In our adaptation-defective 2-DSB strain, the DSBs are located at "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "MAT"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " (86 kb from "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "CEN3"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ") and near "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "FAB1"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " (42 kb from "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "CEN6"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). Sadeghi et al. employed two strains, both of which escape prolonged G"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Subscript",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "/M arrest, with at least one DSB site far from its centromere; at "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "URA3"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " (36 kb from its centromere) and "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "ADH1"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " (170 kb) or at "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "MIC2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " (32 kb) and "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "DLD2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " (316 kb)."
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "To investigate whether the distance between the second DSB site and the centromere would affect whether cells will remain permanently arrested, we created several strains that contain a second cut site at various distances from the centromere, in addition to the cut site at "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "MAT"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " on chromosome III ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Figure 8"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#fig8",
                  "jatsRefType": "fig"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). Strain YSL53, which has a second DSB at chromosome V, 86 kb away from "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "CEN5"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib25",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Lee et al., 1998"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "), and strain DW417, which has a second DSB 52 kb away from "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "CEN6"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib26",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Lee et al., 2014"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "), mostly remained in G"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Subscript",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "/M arrest 24 hr after the induction of DNA breaks. However, in GEM188, which had a second cut site 230 kb away from the "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "CEN2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ", only 37% of cells remained in G"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Subscript",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "/M arrest by 24 hr. Thus, the increased distance of the second DSB site to the centromere in GEM188 appears to have led to a less robust triggering of the SAC compared with YSL53 and DW417."
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Figure",
              "id": "fig8",
              "label": "8",
              "labelAutomatically": false,
              "caption": [
                {
                  "type": "Paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Strong",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Text",
                          "value": {
                            "string": "Adaptation assay of different 2-DSB strains. "
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "("
                      }
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Strong",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Text",
                          "value": {
                            "string": "A"
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": ") Adaptation assay of 1-DSB and 2-DSB strains tracking the morphology of 50 G"
                      }
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Subscript",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Text",
                          "value": {
                            "string": "1"
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": " cells on a YEP-Gal plate. The percentage of G"
                      }
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Subscript",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Text",
                          "value": {
                            "string": "2"
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "/M-arrested cells was shown 4 hr and 24 hr after placement of YEP-Gal plates. JKM179 is a 1-DSB strain with an HO-cut site in the "
                      }
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Emphasis",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Text",
                          "value": {
                            "string": "MAT"
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": " locus on chromosome III 86 kb away from the centromere. DW417 is a 2-DSB strain derived from JKM179 with an additional HO-cut site on chromosome VI 52 kb away from the centromere. YSL53 is a 2-DSB strain derived from JKM179 with an additional HO-cut site at the "
                      }
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Emphasis",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Text",
                          "value": {
                            "string": "URA3"
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": " locus on chromosome V 36 kb away from the centromere ("
                      }
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Citation",
                      "target": "bib25",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Text",
                          "value": {
                            "string": "Lee et al., 1998"
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "). GEM188 is a 2-DSB strain derived from JKM179 with an additional HO-cut site at "
                      }
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Emphasis",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Text",
                          "value": {
                            "string": "LYS2"
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": " on chromosome II 230 kb away from the centromere. ("
                      }
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Strong",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Text",
                          "value": {
                            "string": "B"
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": ") Cartoon representations of strains showing the location of the HO-cut sites relative to their respective centromeres."
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "ImageObject",
                  "contentUrl": "elife-94334-fig8-v1.tif",
                  "mediaType": "image/tiff"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Our previous data have suggested that the involvement of the SAC in prolonging DSB-induced arrest was dependent on centromere sequences on the broken chromosome and involved post-translational modification of chromatin by the Mec1- and Tel1-dependent phosphorylation of the histone H2A ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib11",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Dotiwala et al., 2010"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). In budding yeast, histone H2B is also targeted by DDC kinases upon DNA damage ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib26",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Lee et al., 2014"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). To test whether the presence of these chromatin modifications around centromeres would be sufficient to elicit a SAC response, we examined cell cycle progression in strains in which both histone H2A and/or histone H2B genes were mutated to their putative phosphomimetic forms (H2A-S129E and H2B-T129E). We note that although histone H2A-S129E is recognized by an antibody specific for the phosphorylation of histone H2A-S129 ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib14",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Eapen et al., 2012"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "), the mutation to S129E may not be fully phosphomimetic. As shown in "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Figure 9"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#fig9",
                  "jatsRefType": "fig"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ", there was no effect on the growth rate of either the single or the double mutants, suggesting that cells did not experience a SAC-dependent delay in entering mitosis because of these modifications."
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Figure",
              "id": "fig9",
              "label": "9",
              "labelAutomatically": false,
              "caption": [
                {
                  "type": "Paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Strong",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Text",
                          "value": {
                            "string": "Phosphomimetic and non-phosphorylatable mutants of histone H2A and H2B do not affect the growth rate of cells. "
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Growth rate of strains were measured in YPD (2% dextrose) in H2A and H2B mutants for up to 10 hr. Cultures were grown in YPD until they reached an OD"
                      }
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Subscript",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "Text",
                          "value": {
                            "string": "600"
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": " of 0.1. The OD of each strain was then measured at 3, 5, 7, and 10 hr."
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "ImageObject",
                  "contentUrl": "elife-94334-fig9-v1.tif",
                  "mediaType": "image/tiff"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "Section",
      "id": "s3",
      "sectionType": "Discussion",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "Heading",
          "level": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "Discussion"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "Here, we studied how cells maintain cell cycle arrest following DNA damage by using a yeast strain that permanently halts cell cycle with two persistent DNA breaks. We find that most of the DDC signaling proteins must remain active to maintain G"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Subscript",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "2"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "/M arrest, highlighting the importance of continuous checkpoint signaling in preventing premature mitotic entry and therefore, genome instability ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Link",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Figure 10"
                  }
                }
              ],
              "target": "#fig10",
              "jatsRefType": "fig"
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": ")."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Figure",
          "id": "fig10",
          "label": "10",
          "labelAutomatically": false,
          "caption": [
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Strong",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Activation and maintenance of checkpoint arrest in response to a DSB. "
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "The Mre11-Rad50-Xrs2 (MRX) complex is one of the first complexes recruited to DSBs and initiates the resection of dsDNA to ssDNA. ssDNA is then coated with RPA which recruits Ddc2. Mec1 is the primary kinase responsible for checkpoint arrest in budding yeast and is activated by Ddc2 and Ddc1 from the 9-1-1 clamp. Proteins in green (Ddc2, Rad9, Rad24, and Rad53) were required for the activation and maintenance of checkpoint arrest. While Chk1 was not required for establishment of G"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Subscript",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "/M arrest, it contributed to the maintenance of arrest. In contrast, Dun1 was required for checkpoint activation but was dispensable 4 hr after DSB induction. Prolonged arrest >24 hr in a 2-DSB strain was dependent on the SAC proteins Mad2, Mad1, and Bub2 as well as the distance between the second HO-cut site and the centromere."
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "ImageObject",
              "contentUrl": "elife-94334-fig10-v1.tif",
              "mediaType": "image/tiff"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "Phosphorylation of Rad53 by Mec1 is tightly linked to cell cycle arrest following DNA damage ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib47",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Pellicioli et al., 2001"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). While Rad53 is also shown to be targeted by Tel1, deleting "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Emphasis",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "TEL1"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": " did not affect the prolonged checkpoint arrest in a 2-DSB strain. This finding supports the previous reports showing that cell cycle arrest in response to enzymatic DNA breaks is largely orchestrated by Mec1, with a minor contribution from Tel1 ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib47",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Pellicioli et al., 2001"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "; "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib60",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Vaze et al., 2002"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). Here, we add that Mec1 inactivation via Ddc2 depletion, 4 hr after DSB induction, results in rapid resumption of the cell cycle, most likely through Ptc2, Ptc3, Pph3, and Glc7-dependent dephosphorylation and inactivation of the DDC signaling protein Rad53 ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib5",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Bazzi et al., 2010"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "; "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib27",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Leroy et al., 2003"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "; "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib44",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "O’Neill et al., 2007"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). However, the regulation and consequences of Rad53 phosphorylation are apparently more complex, given that cells can re-enter mitosis in the presence of persistent Rad53 phosphorylation when SAC proteins Mad1 and Mad2 are depleted (see below)."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "Mec1 activation in the S and G"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Subscript",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "2"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": " cell cycle phases is achieved by at least two converging mechanisms; first, through Ddc2 binding to RPA-coated ssDNA created the 5’ to 3’ resection of the DSB ends, and second, through binding of the 9-1-1 clamp subunit Ddc1 ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib12",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Dubrana et al., 2007"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "; "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib42",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Navadgi-Patil and Burgers, 2009"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "; "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib68",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Zou and Elledge, 2003"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "; "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib36",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Melo et al., 2001"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). Before Ddc1 can activate Mec1, the 9-1-1 checkpoint clamp must be loaded by the clamp loader, which consists of Rad24-Rfc2-5 ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib36",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Melo et al., 2001"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). DDC activation largely depends on a functional clamp loader as cells lacking "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Emphasis",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "RAD24"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": " proceed directly into mitosis in response to a single DSB, with only a brief delay ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib3",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Aylon and Kupiec, 2003"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). If the clamp loader acts just once to load the 9-1-1 clamp and the clamp then slides away from the DSB as DNA is resected, then removal of Rad24 after the checkpoint had been robustly activated should not perturb arrest. However, we find that Rad24 depletion leads to rapid cell cycle resumption in response to 2 DSBs, suggesting that multiple 9-1-1 clamp loading events are required to sustain extended cell cycle arrest. We posit that 9-1-1 clamp may require continuous reloading as the 5’ end is being continuously resected by Exo1 and Sgs1-Rmi1-Top3-Dna2 exonucleases ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib67",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Zhu et al., 2008"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": ")."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "The adaptor kinase Rad9, downstream of Mec1 and Tel1, is responsible for scaffolding and activating the effector DDC kinases Rad53 and Chk1 ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib16",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Emili, 1998"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "; "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib52",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Schwartz et al., 2002"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "; "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib56",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Sweeney et al., 2005"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). Here, we show that conditional depletion of Rad9 shortly after the induction of 2 DSBs prompts mitotic re-entry and terminates the DDC, evident from rapid Rad53 dephosphorylation. Thus, Rad9 is required for continued maintenance of Rad53 phosphorylation."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "Depletion of Rad53-AID 4 hr after inducing 2 DSBs triggers release of cells from G"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Subscript",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "2"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "/M arrest, albeit resumption of mitosis is delayed by 4 hr compared to resumption of mitosis seen when upstream DDC factors Ddc2, Rad9, or Rad24 are depleted. We discovered that this residual delay in mitotic re-entry is dependent on Chk1 signaling. We postulate that Chk1 may delay cell cycle re-entry in the absence of Rad53 by phosphorylating and stabilizing Pds1 ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib1",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Agarwal et al., 2003"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": ")."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "Agreeing with previously published work ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib63",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Yam et al., 2020"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "), we find that deleting "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Emphasis",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "DUN1"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": " resulted in a less robust DDC activation and a shortened G"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Subscript",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "2"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "/M arrest. However, unlike the depletion of other DDC proteins examined in this study, depletion of Dun1-AID 4 hr after DSB induction was not sufficient to promote cell cycle re-entry. It is possible that the transcripts upregulated by Dun1 after DNA damage ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib65",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Zhao and Rothstein, 2002"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "; "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib66",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Zhou and Elledge, 1993"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": ") are stable for several hours and are sufficient to sustain the arrest even in the absence of Dun1. We also find that cell cycle arrest upon depletion of Dun1 is Chk1-dependent. These results are in consistent with Dun1’s role in stabilizing Pds1 through a Chk1-independent mechanism ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib63",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Yam et al., 2020"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": ")."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "Here, we show that prolonged cell cycle arrest following induction of 2 DSBs becomes independent of DDC proteins Ddc2, Rad9, and Rad53, but dependent on SAC proteins Mad1 and Mad2. Depletion of Mad1 or Mad2 4 hr after DSB induction did not immediately result in cell cycle re-entry, but around 15 hr cells began to resume cell cycle. The timing of cell cycle re-entry for Mad1/2-depleted cells following the induction of 2 DSB is about the same as the timing of cell cycle re-entry in response to a single DNA break in wild-type cells. Even when DDC signaling is artificially upregulated via Ddc2 overexpression, cells re-enter mitosis if Mad2 is depleted. Surprisingly, in the absence of Mad1 or Mad2 cells escaped arrest despite persistent Rad53 hyperphosphorylation, suggesting that cells become ‘deaf’ to the DDC signal once SAC takes over."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "Previous work indicates that SAC proteins contribute to the DNA damage response ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib11",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Dotiwala et al., 2010"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "; "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib18",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Garber and Rine, 2002"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "; "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib22",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Kim and Burke, 2008"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). Work from our lab has suggested that γ-H2AX spreading from a DSB to the centromere of the same chromosome might impair kinetochore attachment and thus trigger a SAC response ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib11",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Dotiwala et al., 2010"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). SAC signaling is not sufficient to elicit a permanent cell cycle arrest in response to a single DSB; however, we find that inducing 2 DSBs, each within 100 kb of its centromere, elicits a SAC-dependent permanent arrest. As strength of the SAC has a direct relation to the number of unattached kinetochores ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib9",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Dick and Gerlich, 2013"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "), the addition of a second DSB on another chromosome might trigger a stronger SAC response and result in a permanent cell cycle arrest."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "The fact that not all combinations of 2 DSBs produce permanent arrest ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib50",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Sadeghi et al., 2022"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": ") can be explained by the idea that the distance between the DSB site and its corresponding centromere is an important determinant of the extent of cell cycle arrest. We previously observed that a strain with a single DSB 200 kb away from the centromere had shorter cell cycle arrest compared to a strain with a DSB 86 kb away from the centromere ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib11",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Dotiwala et al., 2010"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). Here, we provide further evidence suggesting that the distances between DSBs and their corresponding centromeres determine whether SAC will be fully activated to prolong the arrest. Our previous results showed that when "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Emphasis",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "MAD2"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": " was deleted, the length of cell cycle arrest was the same in strains with a single DSB, irrespective of the DSB’s distance to its centromere ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib11",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Dotiwala et al., 2010"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). We suggest that the strains used by "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib50",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Sadeghi et al., 2022"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": " do not remain permanently arrested because one of the two DSBs in their strains is sufficiently far from its centromere to fully trigger SAC."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "In addition to blocking the metaphase to anaphase transition, components of the SAC also block mitotic exit (reviewed by "
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib33",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Matellán and Monje-Casas, 2020"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). We examined the role of Bub2/Bfa1 heterodimer, the most upstream components of the MEN pathway ("
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "Citation",
              "target": "bib33",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Matellán and Monje-Casas, 2020"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "). Much like Mad1 and Mad2, we found that neither Bub2 nor Bfa1 was required for the establishment of cell cycle arrest in response to DNA damage; but our study revealed a surprising result: Bub2, but not its partner Bfa1, is essential to prolong cell cycle arrest, indicating that Bub2 has a Bfa1-independent role."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "By using conditional depletion of various proteins that contribute to cell cycle arrest, we show that the establishment, maintenance, and inactivation stages of DNA damage-provoked cell cycle arrest involve different sets of factors following DNA damage. After the DNA damage checkpoint is established, its maintenance proves to be divided into two distinct phases. Arrest up to about 15 hr requires the constant presence of most of the identified DDC proteins, including Ddc2, Rad9, Rad24, and Rad53, with Dun1 playing an important but nonessential role. Although Chk1 was not required either to establish or initially to maintain cell cycle arrest, its absence shortened arrest, most notably when Rad53 was depleted. Surprisingly, neither Ddc2, Rad9, nor Rad53 (and we suggest likely Rad24) are necessary for the prolongation of cell cycle arrest lasting longer than 15–24 hr. Instead, this prolonged arrest is enforced by SAC proteins Mad1, Mad2, and Bub2."
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "Section",
      "id": "s4",
      "sectionType": "Materials",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "Heading",
          "level": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Text",
              "value": {
                "string": "Materials and methods"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Section",
          "id": "s4-1",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Heading",
              "level": 2,
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Yeast strain and plasmid construction"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "All AID-tagged mutant strains were derived from a modified version of strains JKM179. To create the strain with two HO cleavage sites (DW417), an HO-cut site, designated HOcse6, with an adjacent HPH marker was integrated into chromosome VI, 52 kb from the centromere. To create AID strains, we first integrated osTIR1 at "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "URA3"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " after digesting plasmid pNHK53 ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib43",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Nishimura et al., 2009"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ") with "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Stu"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "I. To integrate osTIR1-F74G at "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "URA3"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ", the plasmid pMK420 ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib64",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Yesbolatova et al., 2020"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ") was digested with "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Stu"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "I. For degron-tagging of DDC proteins, AID-9xMyc (AID) PCR products were generated with mixed oligos with homology to the C-terminal end of the corresponding open-reading frames by using plasmids pKan–AID–9xMyc (pJH2892) or pNat–AID–9xMyc (pJH2899) as templates ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib40",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Morawska and Ulrich, 2013"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). Deletion of ORFs and insertion of AID tags were introduced with the one-step PCR homology cassette amplification and the standard yeast transformation method ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib61",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Wach et al., 1994"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). Cas9 editing was done by inserting a gRNA into plasmid bRA90 ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib2",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Anand et al., 2017"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ") and co-transformed into our strain of interest with a donor sequence. Transformants were verified by PCR, western blotting, and sequencing. To create strain GEM188 with 2-DSBs, we inserted a second HO-cut site into JKM179 at "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "LYS2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " locus by CRISPR/Cas9 ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib2",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Anand et al., 2017"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ") using a synthetic DNA template with 117 bp consensus HO recognition site. Non-phosphorylatable and phosphomimetic mutants of H2A were generated in a JKM179 background using CRISPR/Cas9 to target HTA1 and HTA2 genes at serine 129 and 80 nt templates to mutate serine to either alanine (non-phosphorylatable) or glutamic acid (phosphomimetic). H2B mutants were generated in a JKM179 background using CRISPR/Cas9 to target "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "HTB1"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " and "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "HTB2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " genes at threonine 129 with 80 nt repair templates to mutate threonine to either alanine or glutamic acid."
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "The "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "CEN/ARS"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " plasmid pFZ052-"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "pRAD9-AID-*9Myc::Trp1"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " (pRAD9-AID) was obtained by digesting the plasmid pFL36.1 ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib24",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Lazzaro et al., 2008"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ") with "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "SmaI"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " and "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "AscI"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " to excise the "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "3"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " HA tag on the C-terminal end of Rad9. A "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "9xMyc-AID"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " PCR product generated from the plasmid pJH2892 (pKan-9xMyc-AID) was cut with "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "AscI"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " and sticky/blunt end cloned into the "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "SmaI-AscI"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "-digested pFL36.1 to add the "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Emphasis",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "9xMyc-AID"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " tag to the C-terminal end of Rad9. pRad9-AID was retained by growing cells in a Trp- media with 2% raffinose. The primers used for strain and plasmid creation are listed in the Key Resources Table, "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Supplementary file 1a"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#supp1",
                  "jatsRefType": "supplementary-material"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ", and "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Supplementary file 1b"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#supp1",
                  "jatsRefType": "supplementary-material"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ". Plasmids are listed in the Key Resources Table."
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Section",
          "id": "s4-2",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Heading",
              "level": 2,
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Culturing conditions, HO expression, and auxin treatment"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Strains containing degron fusions and galactose-inducible HO were cultured using standard procedures. Briefly, a single colony grown on a YEPD plate (1% yeast extract, 2% peptone, 2% dextrose, 2.5% agar) was inoculated in 5 ml YEP-lactate (YEP containing 3% lactic acid) and was grown for ~15 hr at 30°C with agitation. Next day, the overnight culture was used to inoculate a 500–100 ml of YEP-lactate culture such that the cell density reached an OD"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Subscript",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "600"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " of 0.5 the following day. After harvesting 15 ml liquid culture before treatment, HO expression was induced by galactose treatment with a 2% final concentration. Then, cultures were split either at 4 hr or 15 hr following induction with galactose. The split cultures were treated either with IAA or 5-Ph-IAA or an equivalent volume of 200 proof ethanol. IAA (Sigma-Aldrich, I3750) was resuspended in ethanol for a 500 mM stock and used at a 1 mM final concentration both for liquid media and for agar plates. 5-Ph-IAA was dissolved in ethanol for a 1 mM stock and used at a final concentration of 1 µM. 15 ml of liquid culture were harvested at various timepoints and prepared for microscopy or western blot analysis as described below."
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "To measure growth rate, strains were grown in 5 ml of YEPD with 2% dextrose at 30°C with an initial OD"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Subscript",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "600"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " of 0.1. The OD was measured 3, 5, 7, and 10 hr after the initial OD measurement using a Thermo Scientific NanoDrop 2000c Spectrophotometer. To measure the OD, 50 µl of culture was added to 950 µl of fresh YPD in a cuvette at a dilution of 1:20."
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Section",
          "id": "s4-3",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Heading",
              "level": 2,
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "TCA protein extraction"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Protein extracts were prepared for western blot analysis by TCA extraction protocol as previously explained ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib38",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Miller-Fleming et al., 2014"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). Briefly, 15 ml of liquid culture was spun down and the media was discarded. Harvested cells were incubated on ice in 1.5 ml microcentrifuge tubes with 20% TCA for 20 min. Cells were washed with acetone and the pellet was air-dried. 200 µl of MURBs buffer (50 mM sodium phosphate, 25 mM MES, 3 M urea, 0.5% 2-mercaptoethanol, 1 mM sodium azide, and 1% SDS) was added to each sample with acid-washed glass beads. Cells were lysed by mechanical shearing with glass beads for 2 min. Crude cell lysates were harvested by poking a hole in the bottom of the 1.5 ml microcentrifuge tube and spinning the tubes on a 15 ml conical tube. Samples were boiled at 95°C for 10 min prior to loading on SDS-PAGE."
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Section",
          "id": "s4-4",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Heading",
              "level": 2,
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Western blotting"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "8–20 µl of denatured protein samples prepared by TCA extraction were loaded onto a 10% or 8% SDS-PAGE gels. Proteins were separated by applying constant voltage at 90 V until the 37 kDa protein standard band reached the bottom of the gel. Gels were transferred to an Immun-Blot PVDF membrane (Bio-Rad) using a wet transfer apparatus set to 100 V constant voltage for 1 hr at 4°C. Membranes were then blocked with OneBlock blocking buffer (Genesee Scientific, Cat# 20-313) for 1 hr at room temperature, After three 10 min washes with 1× TBS-T, blots were incubated with either anti-Myc [9E11] (Abcam, ab56) to detect TIR1 and AID fusions, anti-Rad53 [EL7.E1] (Abcam, ab166859), anti-Pgk1 (Abcam, ab30359), or anti-Rad9 ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib59",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Usui et al., 2009"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": ") for 1 hr at room temperature or at 4°C overnight. Blots were washed three times with 1× TBS-T and incubated with anti-mouse HRP (GE Healthcare, Cat# NXA931) or anti-rabbit HRP secondary antibody (Sigma-Aldrich, Cat# A6154) for 1 hr at room temperature. After washing the membranes three times with 1× TBS-T, Amersham ECL Prime Western Blotting Detection Reagent was added to fully coat the blots and left to incubate for 5 min at room temperature with gentle agitation. Blots were imaged using a Bio-Rad ChemiDoc XR+ imager and prepared for publication using ImageLab 6.1 software (Bio-Rad) and Adobe Photoshop CC 2017. The reagents used are listed in the Key Resources Table."
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Section",
          "id": "s4-5",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Heading",
              "level": 2,
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Microscopy, DAPI staining, and cell morphology determination"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Aliquots from YEP-Lac cultures were taken either 4 hr or 15 hr after adding galactose, diluted 20-fold in sterile water, and plated on a YEP-Agar with 2% galactose with or without 1 mM IAA or 1 µM 5-Ph-IAA. Cells were counted on a light microscope with a ×10 objective, examined, and binned into three categories: unbudded, small buds, and G"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Subscript",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "/M-arrested cells with large buds. For each timepoint, >250 cells were analyzed. For DAPI staining, 450 µl of culture was added to 50 µl of 37% formaldehyde and incubated in the chemical hood at room temperature for 20 min. Samples were spun down at 8000 rpm for 5 min and washed with 1× PBS three times. Cells were resuspended in 50 µl of DAPI mounting media (VECTASHIELD Antifade Mounting Medium with DAPI H-1200-10) and incubated at room temperature for 10 min, away from direct light. The samples were imaged by using a Nikon Ni-E upright microscope equipped with a Yokogawa CSU-W1 spinning-disk head, an Andor iXon 897U EMCCD camera, Nikon Elements AR software, a ×60 oil immersion objective, and a 358 nm laser. Fifteen z-stacks with a thickness of 0.3 µm were collected per image. In the morphology assays, at least three biological replicates were used for each strain."
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Section",
          "id": "s4-6",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Heading",
              "level": 2,
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Adaptation and auxin plating assays"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "We performed adaptation assays as previously described ("
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib14",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Eapen et al., 2012"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "; "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Citation",
                  "target": "bib25",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Lee et al., 1998"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "). Cells grown in YEP-Lac overnight were diluted 20-fold in sterile water and plated on a YEP-agar plate containing 2% galactose. Using micromanipulation, 50 G"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Subscript",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "1"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " cells were isolated and positioned in a grid followed by incubation at 30°C. To quantify the percentage of adapted cells, the number of cells that re-entered cell cycle and grew to a microcolony (3+ cells) after 24 hr was divided by the total number of cells. For auxin plating assays, damage was induced in a YEP-Lac liquid culture by adding galactose at a final concentration of 2%, as described above. Cells were then transferred onto YEP-agar plates containing 2% galactose and 1 mM IAA or 1 µM 5-Ph-IAA 4 hr or 15 hr after adding galactose. For each timepoint, >250 cells were scored and categorized as described above for the adaptation assay from at least three biological replicates."
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "Section",
          "id": "s4-7",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "Heading",
              "level": 2,
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Quantification and data analysis"
                  }
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "Paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "Graphs were prepared using GraphPad Prism 10 (Dotmatics). Statistical analysis for differences in the percentage of large budded (G"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Subscript",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "2"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": "/M-arrested) cells at different timepoints listed in "
                  }
                },
                {
                  "type": "Link",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "Text",
                      "value": {
                        "string": "Table 1"
                      }
                    }
                  ],
                  "target": "#table1",
                  "jatsRefType": "table"
                },
                {
                  "type": "Text",
                  "value": {
                    "string": " was done using a one-way ANOVA in GraphPad Prism 10. Protein quantification of Ddc2-myc blots was done using ImageLab 6.1 (Bio-Rad). To categorize DAPI-stained cells based on their morphology and number of DAPI signals, images were captured as described above and viewed using ImageJ with the Fiji addon."
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
